{
  "image_name": "bf037e20508df3c443ffc85fa5cc516f.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares four manufacturing routes for battery electrodes and highlights how each turns active material into a finished electrode. The first route is conventional slurry-based processing: electrode components are dispersed in a liquid binder solution, coated onto a current collector, dried by solvent evaporation, and then compressed by calendering. This is presented as the standard wet process.\n\nIn contrast, dry processing avoids solvent. Dry powders are mixed, compressed directly into a free-standing film, and then laminated onto a current collector. The emphasis is on eliminating the solvent evaporation step while still forming a dense electrode layer.\n\nA third route, beam-curing processing, also starts from a slurry-like dispersion, but instead of relying mainly on drying, the deposited electrode is cured using radiation before calendering. This suggests a different way to solidify and consolidate the coating.\n\nThe final route is 3D printing, where an ink or extruded filament is prepared and dispensed in patterned lines onto a substrate. After printing, the structure undergoes post-treatments such as heat drying, freeze drying, or NaOH rinsing.\n\nOverall, the figure shows a progression from traditional coating to solvent-free, radiation-assisted, and additive manufacturing approaches for electrode fabrication.",
